WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:06.534
From a World War II film about a dressed-up corpse&nbsp;
to a creepy horror comedy about a satanic child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.534 --> 00:00:10.991
Netflix has a whole slew of hidden gems&nbsp;
that are just waiting to be discovered.

00:00:10.991 --> 00:00:15.840
The best science-fiction tales aren't just&nbsp;
about unsure futures and alien worlds — they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.280
also use new and unfamiliar environments to&nbsp;
point out universal truths about the human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.900
condition. The script for Advantageous&nbsp;
is that kind of story. The film is set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.220
in a technologically-advanced New York City&nbsp;
of tomorrow, where the divide between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.060
rich and the poor is more pronounced than&nbsp;
ever. As a result of this class struggle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.320
mothers will do absolutely anything to make&nbsp;
sure their children get an upper hand in life.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.720
For Gwen, a wealthy cosmetic procedure salesperson&nbsp;
who gets fired right as her daughter enters an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.880
expensive school, that means agreeing to be a test&nbsp;
subject for a painful procedure. The procedure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.740
involves having her consciousness transferred&nbsp;
entirely into a new body. Complications arise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.360
calling into question whether or not this new&nbsp;
Gwen is even Gwen at all. In its review of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.400
Advantageous, The Magazine of Fantasy and Science&nbsp;
Fiction called the film "quiet and affecting."

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.920
A young woman named Alice secretly&nbsp;
performs explicit acts as Lola_Lola,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.240
camgirl extraordinaire. Though she's willing&nbsp;
to do whatever it takes to rise through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.380
ranks of popularity on her seedy hosting&nbsp;
site, Alice is able to keep an emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.760
distance from her work — that is until a&nbsp;
dark contingent encourages her to perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.860
a shocking act of violence. In his review of&nbsp;
the film, Ty Burr of The Boston Globe wrote:

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.960
"Cam plays with both psychological horror&nbsp;
genre tropes and darker existential themes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.620
and its clear-eyed view of the drab&nbsp;
realities of a camgirl's life is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.184
both refreshing and ultra-creepy."

00:01:37.184 --> 00:01:39.523
A coming-of-age story about people 

00:01:39.523 --> 00:01:43.740
just trying to find their place in the world,&nbsp;
Dumplin' boasts a cast led by comedy legend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:49.560
Jennifer Aniston and charismatic star-on-the-rise&nbsp;
Danielle Macdonald. The latter plays Willowdean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.420
Dickson. She prefers to be called Will, but her&nbsp;
mother insists on referring to her as Dumplin'.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.400
A supremely confident and witty teenager,&nbsp;
Dumplin' stands up for herself against bullies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.660
who criticize her for her weight. Standing&nbsp;
up to her mother, however, is a lot harder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.620
So what better way to establish herself than&nbsp;
competing in her mother's beloved Miss Teen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.120
Bluebonnet Pageant? What initially begins as&nbsp;
a protest against pageants and her mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.440
soon becomes a celebration of all types&nbsp;
of beauty, thus prompting other overlooked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:20.880
misfits to join Dumplin'. In her review of&nbsp;
the film, Elizabeth Weitzman of TheWrap wrote:

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.200
"This is a generous movie, and an&nbsp;
unrepentant fantasy at that, so it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.101
hard not to meet it with open heartedness."

00:02:28.101 --> 00:02:30.946
Operation Mincemeat tells a fascinating and 

00:02:30.946 --> 00:02:35.280
gripping but little-known World War II&nbsp;
story, one so filled with audacious and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.000
foolhardy participants that it's hard&nbsp;
to believe it's real. Set in 1943,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.520
the Allied forces plan to stop Hitler's conquest&nbsp;
of Europe with a multi-pronged attack on Sicily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:49.380
a troubling option because it would ensure&nbsp;
mass casualties on both sides. And so, two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:54.534
elite British intelligence officers come up with&nbsp;
a bizarre scheme, known as "Operation Mincemeat."

00:02:54.534 --> 00:02:58.740
"Our other deception plans are&nbsp;
strategies designed to confuse the enemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.220
throw him off balance. Mincemeat, on&nbsp;
the other hand, is an outright lie."

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:07.140
With plenty of help from higher-ups and the&nbsp;
rank-and-file, they dress up an abandoned corpse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:12.180
in a Royal Marines uniform and plant evidence&nbsp;
of a false Allied invasion on it. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.440
the intelligence officers wait for the Axis&nbsp;
forces to discover the dressed-up corpse and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:21.660
act accordingly on the misinformation. In his&nbsp;
rave of the film, Jake Wilson of The Age wrote:

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:25.740
"'Operation Mincemeat' is a genuine&nbsp;
surprise package — it prompts nostalgia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.733
if not for the war, then for an almost equally&nbsp;
distant era of skillfully crafted entertainment."

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:37.740
After expelling Belgian colonialists in 1960,&nbsp;
the Congo declared independence. So in 1961,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:42.600
the United Nations arrived to help support the&nbsp;
newly formed government. U.N. peacekeeping troops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.860
soon got caught up in a civil war between&nbsp;
the Democratic Republic of the Congo and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.420
the natural resource-heavy secessionist&nbsp;
state of Katanga. The Siege of Jadotville&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:56.220
tells of a little-known moment in this seven-day&nbsp;
conflict, in which 3,000 mercenaries working for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.380
mining companies in Katanga attacked the&nbsp;
150 Irish troops serving under the U.N.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:05.340
Led by commandant Pat Quinlan, the&nbsp;
defense held its ground, pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:10.800
back the thousands of attackers while waiting for&nbsp;
reinforcements to arrive. The film is a harrowing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.900
high-stakes drama, loaded with military action and&nbsp;
almost unbelievable acts of courage and tactical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:20.640
brilliance. If you're a fan of war movies,&nbsp;
The Siege of Jadotville is not to be missed.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:25.800
The fear of turning into one's parents as&nbsp;
they settle into adulthood is an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:30.480
common worry — it's also the basis for the&nbsp;
psychological-supernatural horror film Umma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.500
Amanda and her teenage daughter Chrissy&nbsp;
enjoy an idyllic existence on their farm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.540
hanging out and tending to bees. However,&nbsp;
everything changes when the remains of Amanda's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:44.940
mother arrive from Korea. Umma wasn't a good mom —&nbsp;
she was cruel and abusive — and it was the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.920
for a long estrangement. But what remains&nbsp;
of her bodily form literally haunts Amanda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:55.320
as she quickly evolves into her mother at her&nbsp;
most monstrous and horrific. While critics found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.880
the film to be uneven, Umma is still worth&nbsp;
a watch. The film is original and quirky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.498
while its unpredictable plot and character&nbsp;
choices subvert many horror movie cliches.

00:05:04.498 --> 00:05:09.840
Dolemite is My Name may have been a huge&nbsp;
critical success when it was released in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.380
but the movie's snubbing by the&nbsp;
Academy at that year's Oscars was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.900
nothing less than scandalous.&nbsp;
So what's all the fuss about?

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:22.020
Dolemite is My Name tells the true story of Rudy&nbsp;
Ray Moore, comedian and Blaxploitation actor known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.760
for his Dolemite movies. The movie covers Moore's&nbsp;
life during the period right before he became a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:31.800
star through to when his first film opens in Los&nbsp;
Angeles. And Eddie Murphy, in a comeback of sorts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:37.140
does a truly outstanding job as Moore. According&nbsp;
to screenwriters Scott Alexander and Larry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:41.280
Karaszewski, Murphy was their only choice for&nbsp;
the role, and they wrote the script with him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.080
in mind. The three had met way back in 2003 and&nbsp;
bonded over their love of the real-life Moore.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:53.820
"And tell him I want him out of here in&nbsp;
24 hours. And 23 of 'em are already gone."

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:56.460
Murphy's co-stars turn in some fine performances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:00.840
too. Wesley Snipes is wonderful playing&nbsp;
the director of Dolemite, D'Urville Martin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:05.700
and Da'Vine Joy Randolph is tremendous as&nbsp;
Lady Reed, Moore's long-time friend and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:10.140
confidant. And then there are the costumes,&nbsp;
designed by Oscar-winner Ruth E. Carter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:16.020
which are as much the stars of Dolemite as the&nbsp;
actors themselves. Swagger, thy name is Dolemite.

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:20.880
Written and directed by Macon Blair, I Don't&nbsp;
Feel at Home in this World Anymore is a comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.020
thriller starring Melanie Lynskey and Elijah&nbsp;
Wood. The movie is about a woman, recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.520
robbed of a laptop, who joins her oddball neighbor&nbsp;
in trying to track it down when the police won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.800
do anything about it. They quickly end up in way&nbsp;
over their heads, and mayhem inevitably ensues.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:40.140
Blair's double duty as writer and director works&nbsp;
well here. He's crafted a tight standalone movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:44.400
that feels like a throwback to the sort of&nbsp;
low-budget, quirky independent features that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.780
so rarely get attention these days. Lynskey's&nbsp;
performance as Ruth is pretty terrific too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:53.580
bringing life to the "everywoman" who has finally&nbsp;
had enough. And then there's Wood, who has, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.680
past few years, excelled at playing the weirdo&nbsp;
— and his role as Tony here is no exception.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.060
Based on Bernard Cornwell's The Saxon&nbsp;
Stories, the Netflix series The Last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:08.040
Kingdom is a historical drama about King Alfred&nbsp;
the Great. The show blends actual events from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:13.260
the 9th century with fiction in order to tell the&nbsp;
story of Alfred's unification of the Anglo-Saxon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:17.760
kingdoms. At the outset of the series, this land&nbsp;
has been conquered and captured by the Vikings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.300
with only King Alfred and his Kingdom&nbsp;
of Wessex standing in their way.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.780
In particular, The Last Kingdom&nbsp;
follows Uhtred of Bebbanburg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:29.940
a noble Saxon who is kidnapped as a slave and&nbsp;
then raised by the Danes. Uhtred is subsequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:33.060
forced to choose between the people that&nbsp;
raised him and the land of his birth.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.140
The Last Kingdom has everything you'd&nbsp;
want in a historical drama covering this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:41.220
time period — brutal fighting, great&nbsp;
characters, intriguing storylines and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:45.540
some gorgeous cinematography. And all those&nbsp;
English accents? Well, they're just a bonus.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.640
Netflix made its first major foray into&nbsp;
original films when it acquired Beasts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.520
No Nation. Directed by True Detective vet Cary&nbsp;
Fukunaga,it tells the harrowing, powerful tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:02.340
of a West African child caught in a civil war and&nbsp;
recruited to a rebel militia as a child soldier.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:06.960
Idris Elba gives a commanding—and&nbsp;
terrifying—performance as the militia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.734
leader who orders his soldiers to&nbsp;
perform ever more horrific acts.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:15.240
Another winner from Netflix's&nbsp;
stable of original films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:19.740
The Fundamentals of Caring takes the "road&nbsp;
movie" formula and gives it an offbeat twist.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:26.760
“Ahhhhh, help me, help me, Ahhhhh!”

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:31.980
Paul Rudd and Craig Roberts team up&nbsp;
as, respectively,a caretaker and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:36.060
teen afflicted with muscular dystrophy&nbsp;
out to explore the country and themselves&nbsp;

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:39.240
(while picking up a hitchhiking&nbsp;
Selena Gomez along the way).

00:08:39.418 --> 00:08:44.578
Writer-director Rob Burnett's deft touch with the&nbsp;
material makes for an enjoyably diverting journey.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.700
It’s a story you’ll probably&nbsp;
feel like you’ve heard before:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.500
a nuclear reactor goes haywire after a&nbsp;
devastating earthquake, endangering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:57.240
citizens of a small South Korean town. As various&nbsp;
government officials try to escape the blame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:01.680
the ordinary folk do their best to avoid the&nbsp;
cataclysmic destruction of the reactor itself.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.260
So far, so bland, right? Well, stick around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:08.640
because towards the end Pandora suddenly&nbsp;
transforms into something so beautiful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:12.480
touching that you can't help but wonder if&nbsp;
Netflix's autoplay switched movies without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.220
you noticing. The rest of Pandora is decent&nbsp;
enough, but that ending is one for the ages.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:24.540
Airplanes are an ideal and almost infinitely&nbsp;
reusable setting for horror movies. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:29.100
a passenger jet is a giant metal box hurtling&nbsp;
through the sky at tremendous and dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:32.640
speeds and disconnected from the regular&nbsp;
world. If there are monsters on a plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.220
it's kind of hard to run away&nbsp;
or call up the authorities.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:40.980
And that's where "Blood Red Sky" begins. At&nbsp;
first, it seems like a standard — if extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:46.260
intense — hijacking drama. But these kinds of&nbsp;
films always have an unlikely hero on board who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:51.720
saves the day. In this case, it's Nadja, a German&nbsp;
single mother seemingly suffering from leukemia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:56.820
who boards a long-haul flight to New York with&nbsp;
her young son, Elias. When terrorists violently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:01.980
take over control of the flight, it's Nadja who&nbsp;
fights back, and it quickly becomes clear that her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:07.260
illness isn't cancer. Rather, it's something&nbsp;
akin to a progressive and aggressive form of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:13.380
vampirism. The result is a bloody good time. Or as&nbsp;
Randy Myers of the San Jose Mercury News put it,

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:18.360
"Director co-writer Peter Thorwarth splatters&nbsp;
the screen with gore while tossing in surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:22.440
delirious preposterousness, and even a&nbsp;
few touching moments. It's grand fun."

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:28.440
An introspective and action-packed MMA tale&nbsp;
both starring and directed by Halle Berry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:29.940
"Bruised" is something special.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:35.040
The Oscar-winner plays Jackie "Pretty Bull"&nbsp;
Justice, a once-great brawler who forfeits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:41.280
a fight and retires a pariah. Still emotionally&nbsp;
smarting from her ignominious exit years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.560
she follows the advice of her boyfriend/manager&nbsp;
and agrees to participate in an underground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:50.820
fight ring that could lead her back to&nbsp;
the top. All of this single-minded focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:55.440
on career and winning gets complicated,&nbsp;
however, when her estranged son reappears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:59.100
on her doorstep. Kevin Maher of The&nbsp;
Times sums "Bruised" up by saying,

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:05.368
"[The film is] rescued at almost every turn&nbsp;
by Berry's fearless, vanity-free turn." 

00:11:05.368 --> 00:11:08.760
"Coffee and Kareem" is about&nbsp;
a clashing, mismatched pair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:12.780
and the film itself is a clashing,&nbsp;
mismatched set of movie styles all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:16.140
ramming into each other. The end&nbsp;
result is full of action, comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:21.720
and even heart. Ed Helms of "The Office" portrays&nbsp;
incompetent Detroit police officer James Coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:26.760
who begins dating single mother Vanessa, much to&nbsp;
the consternation of her 12-year-old son, Kareem.

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:31.740
To scare off the kind and doting Coffee, Kareem&nbsp;
tries to hire a criminal to intimidate the cop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:34.620
but winds up embroiling them both in a massive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:39.240
extremely dangerous conspiracy involving&nbsp;
drug lords and crooked police officers.

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:41.760
"I know it's hard to believe,&nbsp;
but most cops are good people."

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.980
"Spoken like a true white man."

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:49.800
Guillermo del Toro is a masterful writer and&nbsp;
director, specializing in fantasy and horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:55.020
and where those two imaginative genres intersect.&nbsp;
A double Oscar winner for his work on "The Shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.020 --> 00:12:00.480
of Water," del Toro also made emerging classics&nbsp;
like "Pan's Labyrinth" and "Hellboy." But in 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:05.520
he directed a lesser-known fright fest called&nbsp;
"Crimson Peak," a gothic tale that didn't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.380
much love when it came out. Even still, this&nbsp;
haunted house story is definitely worth a watch.

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:15.720
In 19th-century Victorian England, a young&nbsp;
American woman named Edith marries the dashing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:20.700
Sir Thomas Sharpe and goes off to live in his&nbsp;
sprawling mansion in the English countryside. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:26.160
something doesn't feel quite right about the home&nbsp;
or Sir Thomas' creepy sister. And when Edith's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:32.280
abilities to speak with and see the dead manifest,&nbsp;
she learns the truth behind her spooky visions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:36.840
the family into which she probably shouldn't&nbsp;
have just married. Film writer Richard Crouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:42.240
called the film, quote, "bloody terrifying,"&nbsp;
while Trace Thurman of Bloody Disgusting said,

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:45.565
"You'll be mesmerized by what&nbsp;
you're seeing on screen."

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:50.280
Supernatural menai ce is scary, but&nbsp;
so is the dark side of daily life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:54.480
not to mention horrific trauma.&nbsp;
All those elements converge in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:58.500
"His House," the 2020 debut feature&nbsp;
film from writer-director Remi Weekes.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:04.920
Bol and Rial escape wartime South Sudan and&nbsp;
then take a crowded boat with other refugees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:10.740
making it from France to England, where they're&nbsp;
allowed to stay and given a rotting house to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:16.860
in. Facing openly racist neighbors and threats of&nbsp;
deportation, the couple attempts to embrace their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:22.260
newfound Britishness and make a go of the new&nbsp;
normal. Unfortunately, the house is inhabited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:27.300
by pure evil, and a vengeful witch sends the&nbsp;
duo grasping for their lives and even reality.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:32.280
There's a lot going on in "His House," and&nbsp;
almost all of it will provoke some kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:37.200
of emotional reaction from viewers. Tim&nbsp;
Grierson of Screen International writes,

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:40.920
"The film has much to say about&nbsp;
grief, guilt, assimilation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:47.040
and the ongoing refugee crisis. And on top of&nbsp;
all that, it's also a nifty nerve-jangler."

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:49.140
Robert Daniels of Polygon claims,

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:53.520
"[The film] breathes a fresh voice&nbsp;
into the haunted house subgenre."
 &nbsp;

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:58.200
Based on the comic book of the same name by&nbsp;
Charles Forsman, "I Am Not Okay with This" is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:03.180
story of a 17-year-old girl who is trying to deal&nbsp;
with high school, the death of her dad, and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:08.760
newly discovered superpowers. Sydney realizes that&nbsp;
when she has an emotional outburst, as teenagers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:13.140
are prone to do, she is able to move objects.&nbsp;
In addition to finding out she is telekinetic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:17.460
she is also trying to manage the feelings she&nbsp;
has for both of her friends, Dina and Stan.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:19.140
"Can we please stick together?"

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:22.401
"Duh, I'd never leave you."

00:14:22.401 --> 00:14:28.320
Sophia Lillis is terrific in her role as Sydney.&nbsp;
Since her appearance in 2017's "IT," the young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:32.520
actress has proven herself in projects like&nbsp;
"Gretel and Hansel" and "Uncle Frank," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:37.560
"I Am Not Okay with This" is yet another step in&nbsp;
her rise to stardom. This seven-episode Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:43.140
original series is sure to be easily relatable to&nbsp;
anyone who went through an awkward teenage past.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:49.440
Here's a wildly different and unpredictable movie&nbsp;
that merges together movie genres that often rely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.000
so heavily on tropes that they're overly familiar&nbsp;
and predictable. "In the Shadow of the Moon"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:59.880
is part sci-fi movie, part mystery, and part&nbsp;
serial killer story. The film begins in 1988,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:05.340
when Philadelphia police officer Thomas Lockhart&nbsp;
is desperately trying to find and stop a prolific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:10.020
murderer whose victims seem to lose all of&nbsp;
their blood at once. They find the suspect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:13.980
Rya, but she evades capture by&nbsp;
falling to her apparent death.

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:19.140
Flash forward nine years, and Rya is&nbsp;
killing again. Then another nine years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:24.240
and more murders occur. Thomas becomes&nbsp;
the obsessed detective, researching the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:29.820
case for decades as his quest leads him down the&nbsp;
unexpected crime-fighting path of time travel.

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:32.340
Chris Vognar of the New York Times says,

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:38.700
"['In the Shadow of the Moon' is] never less than&nbsp;
engaging, and it's just about always clever."

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:45.060
Adherents of thie dystopian sci-fi boom of the&nbsp;
2010s — both of the literary and film version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:49.020
stuff like "The Maze Runner" and "The Hunger&nbsp;
Games" — will find a lot to like in "Level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:54.720
16." The action centers around the students at an&nbsp;
extremely bleak, sedate, and colorless residential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:59.520
girls-only boarding school. The enrolled&nbsp;
young women are actually more like prisoners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:04.020
and they've been at this school since they&nbsp;
were infants, when they lived on Level 0. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:10.020
as Sophia and Vivien are 16-years-old and live&nbsp;
on Level 16, they start to question their strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:15.120
lives. It's up to Sophia and Vivien to learn&nbsp;
the true nature of their school — and their very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:19.740
existence — if they want to escape.Film writer&nbsp;
Richard Crouse commented on the film, saying,

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:23.100
"Mixing elements of social&nbsp;
justice, horror and sci fi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:28.200
“Level 16” is a genre movie that delivers&nbsp;
both intellectually and emotionally."

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:34.620
Be careful what you wish for, "Nightbooks" subtly&nbsp;
suggests, because wishes just might be real. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:40.020
at least magic is — and it's a powerful and&nbsp;
oppressive source if placed in the wrong hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:45.120
Alex is a little kid who loves to write scary&nbsp;
stories until other kids call him weird for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:50.640
and he endeavors to never write again. Not long&nbsp;
after, a happily wicked and very stylish witch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:55.860
traps him in her magical, traveling, all-consuming&nbsp;
townhouse and orders him to write her a scary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.860 --> 00:17:00.840
story each night ... or she'll kill him. Alex&nbsp;
teams up with another of the witch's young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:06.540
captives, Yasmin, to survive and find a way out of&nbsp;
the villain's clutches by defeating the powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:11.400
magic that's all around them. Looper's own Matthew&nbsp;
Jackson shared his thoughts on the film, sharing,

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:16.740
"[It's] not just an effective little family horror&nbsp;
adventure, but an ode to weird kids everywhere."

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:19.860
"That's good. Really good. Thanks."

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:26.400
Horror movies are most effective in delivering&nbsp;
truly visceral and bewildered responses from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:31.620
audiences when they speak to deep-seated,&nbsp;
widespread fears. They become relatable on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:37.740
a deep, even subliminal level, and great&nbsp;
horror filmmakers use tropes like ghosts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:42.720
monsters, and other supernatural phenomena&nbsp;
as allegories for what truly can eat us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:47.100
alive. That's what's going on in "No One&nbsp;
Gets Out Alive," a Netflix Original from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:51.138
visual effects creator turned first-time&nbsp;
feature director Santiago Menghini.

00:17:51.138 --> 00:17:56.665
Ambar is an undocumented immigrant living&nbsp;
in a rundown, women-only boarding house in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.665 --> 00:18:01.260
Cleveland. Beyond the physical disrepair,&nbsp;
there's a bigger creepiness to the place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:06.360
which becomes clear when the ghosts of previous&nbsp;
borders start to frighten Ambar. But since she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:11.100
living in the U.S. illegally, she can't seek&nbsp;
out the help of authorities or find refuge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:16.260
elsewhere because she fears deportation. "No&nbsp;
One Gets Out Alive" is a haunted house story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:21.060
but it's really about the plight of recent&nbsp;
immigrants trying to survive in a new country.

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:26.220
"The Old Ways" is a haunting thriller about&nbsp;
haunting things. Partially in English and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:30.720
partially in Spanish, it's a horror movie&nbsp;
that blends traditional and familiar elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:36.240
of classic horror movies with Mexican folklore&nbsp;
and tales of witchcraft. Brigitte Kali Canales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:40.680
portrays Cristina Lopez, a Mexican-American&nbsp;
reporter who heads back to her hometown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:46.380
near Veracruz on the Gulf of Mexico, to research&nbsp;
a story about the area's many witchy legends. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:51.000
information gathering quickly turns from&nbsp;
academic into something much darker and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:56.640
hands-on when a collection of local residents&nbsp;
kidnap and hold her against her will. The Bruja&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.640 --> 00:19:01.200
means to use her powers to rid Cristina of&nbsp;
the demon the group believes possesses her.

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:04.260
Michael Talbot-Haynes of Film Threat praised "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:09.660
Old Ways" as the best exorcism movie&nbsp;
since "The Exorcist," while Deirdre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:15.267
Crimmins of Rue Morgue Magazine found it&nbsp;
to be an all-around good and scary movie.

00:19:15.267 --> 00:19:21.540
"Red Dot" is an action movie broken down into its&nbsp;
most basic elements. It's about two people in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:27.240
unfamiliar environment being chased by an unknown&nbsp;
party trying to kill them for unknown reasons. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:32.400
film starts out with Nadja and David, a couple&nbsp;
expecting a baby but otherwise disconnected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:37.080
who embark on a hiking getaway in snowy&nbsp;
northern Sweden. After some awkward and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:41.940
unsettling encounters with locals, Nadja&nbsp;
and David soon find themselves desperately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:46.140
trying to survive brutally violent and&nbsp;
surreptitious attackers. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:51.600
the deadly game of human hunting all makes sense,&nbsp;
but viewers will enjoy putting together the tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:58.860
pieces. Jade Budowski of Decider says "Red Dot"&nbsp;
is, quote, "tense, disturbing, and well-made."

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:03.420
Dave Franco is primarily known as an actor&nbsp;
in comedic films like "The Disaster Artist"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:09.300
and "21 Jump Street." But as a screenwriter and&nbsp;
first-time director with "The Rental," he shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:14.460
his filmmaking ambitions can veer to the sinister&nbsp;
and the tried-and-true hallmarks of horror.

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.552
*Woman screaming in car*

00:20:17.552 --> 00:20:22.200
"The Rental" explores the slight creepiness&nbsp;
that can come with temporarily living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:26.820
in the home of someone else. It also&nbsp;
takes the idea to its darkest depths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:30.300
exploiting the unsettling feelings&nbsp;
of isolation that can accompany a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:35.040
weekend getaway. Married couple Charlie and&nbsp;
Michelle, along with dating pair Josh and Mina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:40.260
rent a remote beach house for a few days. But&nbsp;
when they arrive, things feel a little off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:46.020
particularly because someone is spying on the&nbsp;
foursome and slowly, carefully hunting them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:52.500
down. And it might not even be the rude, racist&nbsp;
property owner. Wenlei Ma of Australia's News.com,

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:58.380
"Delivering solid thrills and an ugly&nbsp;
portrait of disintegrating relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:01.620
'The Rental' is a clever&nbsp;
meld of drama and horror."

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:05.880
Likewise, Susan Kamyab of This&nbsp;
Chix Flix called the film,

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:11.460
"A solid thriller that subtly goes from&nbsp;
nerve-racking drama to shocking slasher."

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:16.380
"The Ritual" plays off the "lost in the&nbsp;
woods" scenario while introducing new and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:21.480
innovative elements to the well-established&nbsp;
subgenre of scary forest flicks. Four old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:26.640
college buddies seek comfort and connection after&nbsp;
the unexpected death of a fifth friend. They go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:32.640
out hiking in a rural area in Scandinavia and&nbsp;
encounter ancient, poorly understood, and deeply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:37.740
evil forces that wish them harm. "The Ritual,"&nbsp;
according to Alan Scherstuhl of the Village Voice,

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:42.660
"Achieves a tense and devilish power,&nbsp;
leaving you in the dark with these guys."

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:45.600
Katie Walsh of the Los Angeles&nbsp;
Times said of the film,

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:51.240
"[It's] efficient and highly effective in its&nbsp;
style, relying on sound, creepy production design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:57.060
and the men's own fear and misjudgment&nbsp;
to create the sense of pervasive doom."
 &nbsp;

00:21:57.060 --> 00:22:01.980
Not much sleep happens in "The Sleepover"&nbsp;
— it's a heist movie and a kidnapping caper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:06.000
with a switcheroo in that it's the kids who&nbsp;
need to save the grown-ups. It's one of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:11.520
movies where tweens get to stay up all night and&nbsp;
go on a grand adventure. Goofy mother Margot was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:16.440
once an intentional master thief, who quit that&nbsp;
life, entered witness protection, and married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:21.900
her dweeby husband Ron. The past comes back to&nbsp;
haunt her when her old team recruits her — by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:26.580
way of kidnapping her and Ron — and forces her to&nbsp;
help them steal a precious crown from a dignitary.

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:29.100
"Your mom is getting cooler by, like, the second."

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:33.960
When Margot and Ron's kids come home to an&nbsp;
empty house, they figure out their mother's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:37.860
secret life and potential whereabouts&nbsp;
and set out to rescue her with the aid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:40.392
of criminal and spy gadgetry she left behind.

00:22:40.392 --> 00:22:43.232
"Small Crimes" is a small crime movie about people 

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:47.400
on the fringes of society who have seemingly&nbsp;
resigned themselves to life on the edge. It's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:52.320
interesting and unsettling take on the&nbsp;
crime-drama genre. Nikolaj Coster-Waldau&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:57.240
plays a fallen-from-grace police officer just&nbsp;
emerging from prison after serving six years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.240 --> 00:23:02.280
for attempted murder. He wants to get his life&nbsp;
on track and redeem himself with his family, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:08.100
they don't want much to do with him. This helps to&nbsp;
send him headlong back into a world of corruption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:14.220
organized crime, casual violence, and other seedy&nbsp;
elements. Mike D'Angelo of The AV Club said,

00:23:14.220 --> 00:23:19.500
"If the film came across as jaded and cynical,&nbsp;
it might well be hard to take. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:24.120
it seems weary and defeated, as if&nbsp;
some people are simply born to fail."

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:29.280
Netflix definitely knew what it was doing when it&nbsp;
snagged this little story for a screen adaptation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:35.400
Based on a novella from Stephen King's Full&nbsp;
Dark, No Stars anthology, 1922 stars Thomas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:41.340
Jane as Wilfred James, a farmer living in rural&nbsp;
Nebraska during the period leading up to the Great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:47.160
Depression. Money troubles and marital conflict&nbsp;
lead Wilfred to some very dark places—and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:51.780
just goes to show that sometimes, getting away&nbsp;
with a crime is much worse than getting caught.

00:23:52.334 --> 00:24:01.620
"I'd murdered my wife to keep my home. The only&nbsp;
way I was leavin’ it now would be in chains."

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:06.060
Like many of Stephen King's best works,&nbsp;
this is a character-driven horror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:10.080
and strong performances make it a&nbsp;
standout—particularly from Jane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:15.060
who immerses himself in the role so thoroughly&nbsp;
that it's hard to believe this same miserable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:19.740
hay-chewing yokel is the same cosmopolitan&nbsp;
pretty boy from The Sweetest Thing.

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:24.900
"You're never gonna find anyone better than my&nbsp;
brother here. He is the greatest and I am serious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:28.011
Just look at him. Chicks freak on this guy."

00:24:28.011 --> 00:24:32.025
In 2014, filmmaker Patrick Brice set out to 

00:24:32.025 --> 00:24:35.880
answer the question "What's the worst thing that&nbsp;
could happen from meeting someone on Craigslist?"

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:42.120
Without spoiling anything, let's just say that&nbsp;
the answer is something really bad. That's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:46.980
a videographer discovers when he goes to&nbsp;
the home of Josef—played by Mark Duplass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:52.860
who also co-wrote and produced the movie—for&nbsp;
what's supposed to be a documentary project. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:58.260
starts out as the ordinary weirdness of internet&nbsp;
strangers becomes something much more disturbing.

00:24:58.571 --> 00:25:03.371
"Oh that's Peach Fuzz… That’s&nbsp;
just Peach Fuzz. Say hello."

00:25:03.558 --> 00:25:06.978
The result is a movie that more&nbsp;
than lives up to its title.

00:25:07.668 --> 00:25:12.660
With so many blockbusters the big screen&nbsp;
in 2017, you might have missed some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.660 --> 00:25:16.680
smaller adaptations of Stephen King's&nbsp;
works—including this one based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.680 --> 00:25:21.960
1992 novel of the same name. Gerald's Game&nbsp;
centers on a woman named who ends up trapped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:26.400
handcuffed to the bed at her lake house&nbsp;
after her husband has a heart attack. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:31.920
she struggles to find a way to escape, she's&nbsp;
visited by a series of entities—some real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:36.840
some imagined, and some that seem to straddle&nbsp;
the line between this world and the next.

00:25:37.419 --> 00:25:41.838
"Who are you?"

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:47.280
One of Netflix's original feature films,&nbsp;
Gerald's Game was eagerly anticipated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:51.960
hardcore King fans but didn't get the kind of&nbsp;
widespread attention merited by its creativity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.960 --> 00:25:57.840
and star power. Despite the obvious challenges of&nbsp;
holding down an entire two-hour film on her own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:03.360
actress Carla Gugino keeps it riveting,&nbsp;
making this the finest horror movie set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:08.460
almost entirely on a bed since, well, maybe&nbsp;
ever. Unless you count Bed of the Dead.

00:26:09.060 --> 00:26:13.680
Have you ever had a nightmare that comes&nbsp;
true? If so, you might be intrigued by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:17.280
the premise of the film Extinction.&nbsp;
That's exactly what happens to Peter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:21.300
who begins having bad dreams about an&nbsp;
invading force that comes to Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.300 --> 00:26:25.560
threatening the safety of his family and&nbsp;
everything he holds dear. Little does he know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:30.420
that these disturbing nightmares would actually&nbsp;
turn out to be premonitions. When he visits a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:35.280
psychiatrist to get to the root of these dreams,&nbsp;
he encounters another patient who's been suffering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:39.840
from the same night terrors, and he begins to&nbsp;
wonder if there is something bigger going on.

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:44.820
"What if something bad is&nbsp;
coming and this is like a sign?"

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:49.260
Unfortunately, Peter's suspicions&nbsp;
prove to be correct: one night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:54.840
spaceships suddenly descend upon the city and&nbsp;
attack. Peter and his wife manage to snag a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:59.640
weapon from one of the invading soldiers, and&nbsp;
Peter utilizes the knowledge from his visions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:04.200
to protect his family — but his understanding&nbsp;
of what's actually happening can only get them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:08.580
so far. Peter is about to discover that he&nbsp;
was receiving these visions for a reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.580 --> 00:27:12.300
and when he finds out why, the&nbsp;
knowledge turns his life upside down.

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:18.540
A sequel to 2010's Skyline, which followed&nbsp;
survivors of an alien invasion of Los Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:23.580
Beyond Skyline explores another story taking&nbsp;
place at the same time as the original movie.

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:28.800
In this film, Los Angeles police detective&nbsp;
Mark Corley helps his son Trent get out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:32.760
jail just as the invasion begins.&nbsp;
Mark and Trent are estranged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:37.440
but Mark couldn't stand to leave his son&nbsp;
behind. As most of the residents of LA are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:43.020
sucked up into various spaceships by huge beams&nbsp;
of light, Mark leads a group of stray survivors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:47.940
through the subway tunnels to the marina, hoping&nbsp;
to escape to somewhere safe. But, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:52.140
their plan doesn't work out the way they had&nbsp;
hoped. When they finally make it to the marina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:56.400
they're captured by the alien flagship —&nbsp;
and that's when the real adventure begins.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:28:00.180
What's actually happening inside these&nbsp;
ships, and what do these creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:04.560
want from human beings? Mark is about to&nbsp;
stumble on some truly disturbing answers.
 &nbsp;

00:28:05.220 --> 00:28:10.560
In just a few short years, director Yeon Sang-ho's&nbsp;
Train to Busan has become a modern zombie classic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:16.260
With that kind of pedigree, you'd think that&nbsp;
his 2018 fantasy-superhero mashup Psychokinesis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:19.800
would have made more waves, but sadly it&nbsp;
slipped under the current for most people.

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:24.540
When a despondent security guard unknowingly&nbsp;
drinks water tainted by some sort of mystical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:28.500
meteor juice, he suddenly gains the power&nbsp;
to move things around with his mind. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.500 --> 00:28:31.740
his newfound abilities, he sets out to&nbsp;
reconnect with his estranged daughter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:35.760
While it takes awhile to get going,&nbsp;
Psychokinesis rewards viewers with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:39.360
epic finale that makes the whole journey&nbsp;
worthwhile, and proves again that Yeon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:43.320
is as skilled behind the camera as he is&nbsp;
at writing heartfelt, emotional stories.

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:48.180
Fashion designer Tom Ford scripted&nbsp;
and directed Nocturnal Animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:53.520
a mind-bending, and deeply unsettling thriller&nbsp;
in which the plot itself blends, interferes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:58.800
and gets confused with a story being told by the&nbsp;
film's troubled characters. Amy Adams plays Susan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:03.600
an unhappily married art gallery owner who&nbsp;
receives a fascinating novel manuscript called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.600 --> 00:29:08.640
Nocturnal Animals, written by her vindictive&nbsp;
first husband, Edward, played by Jake Gyllenhaal.

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:13.980
The action in the film shifts to depict the book,&nbsp;
a horrifying story about Tony, also portrayed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:19.560
Gyllenhaal, a man whose car breaks down in rural&nbsp;
Texas just before a gang of criminals kidnaps his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:24.840
wife and daughter and leaves him stranded. It's&nbsp;
up to Detective Bobby Andes, a strong-but-silent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:29.340
type to help Tony exact his revenge.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, back in Susan and Edward's world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:33.480
viewers learn about the emotional devastation&nbsp;
that led to the demise of their marriage.

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:36.540
Neil Pond of Parade called Nocturnal Animals an...

00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:41.640
"Arty and grotesquely gorgeous, but hardly an&nbsp;
uplifting tale. [...] Think No Country For Old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:45.284
Men meets CSI: West Texas, by way of Twin Peaks."

00:29:45.284 --> 00:29:47.968
A Monster Calls might sound like the name of a 

00:29:47.968 --> 00:29:52.620
horror movie, but it's actually a Spielberg-like&nbsp;
fantasy. Director J.A. Bayona crafts an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:57.060
allegorical, eye-popping film about all-too&nbsp;
real and difficult emotions from the point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.060 --> 00:30:01.920
of a view of a child, which makes it all the more&nbsp;
heartbreaking. A Monster Calls is about grief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:06.240
but it's also about a walking, talking,&nbsp;
tree creature with the voice of Liam Neeson.

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:10.440
Conor is 13, and his mother is dying. He's&nbsp;
just old enough to know what's going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:14.340
but young enough to have no idea how&nbsp;
to cope; he can't turn to his stern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:18.780
grandmother for help and his father isn't&nbsp;
a big part of his life. An unlikely and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.780 --> 00:30:23.460
impossible ally arrives just when Conor&nbsp;
needs him: He is the Monster, a massive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:28.800
sentient yew tree who offers to tell Conor three&nbsp;
grand tales. After that, Conor must share his own.

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:33.300
"Stories are wild creatures, Conor&nbsp;
O'Malley. When you let them loose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:35.160
who knows what havoc they may wreak?"

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:38.880
It's all a little frightening and also&nbsp;
wonderful, and the revelation of where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:43.320
the tree learned his stories will definitely&nbsp;
leave the viewer misty-eyed, which is rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:43.320 --> 00:30:48.060
for a movie about a giant monster who may or&nbsp;
may not be a figment of a child's imagination.

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:53.640
Television has been graced with many reality and&nbsp;
game shows that pit a group of strangers against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.640 --> 00:30:59.340
each other, requiring a vote to eliminate all but&nbsp;
one last-standing champion. Alliances are formed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:59.340 --> 00:31:04.800
but it's ultimately every person for themselves.&nbsp;
The provocative and frightening 2015 film Circle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:11.280
takes the idea and ups the stakes considerably. 50&nbsp;
strangers, all facing execution, are placed in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:15.840
darkened room and made to choose among themselves&nbsp;
which one of them get to walk out alive.

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:17.040
"It's us."

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:17.700
"Us?"

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:18.660
"What do you mean?"

00:31:20.460 --> 00:31:21.660
"We're choosing who dies."

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:25.860
The sparse film's sparse 87&nbsp;
minutes fly by as various unnamed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:30.600
desperate characters passionately argue and&nbsp;
bargain on behalf of their own futures while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:35.220
time and chances run out. Of course, they&nbsp;
also have to fear immediate eradication by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:38.400
the wicked forces holding them in&nbsp;
the room if they so much as move.

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:41.700
John DeFore of The Hollywood Reporter&nbsp;
wrote about the film, saying...

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:46.200
"[Circle] keeps suspense alive, building&nbsp;
to a final tableau that, silently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:50.160
says nearly as much about human nature&nbsp;
as does all the talk leading up to it."
 &nbsp;

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:55.260
An elite squad of secret operatives hunting down&nbsp;
and punishing those who committed unspeakable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.260 --> 00:32:00.480
acts of terror against their community is a&nbsp;
small film genre unto itself — and a good one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:05.520
at that. Among such stories, one can find as&nbsp;
much action and drama as cathartic psychology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:09.840
and shocking real-world history. In the&nbsp;
same vein of Steven Spielberg's Munich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:13.980
which told the story of Israeli Mossad&nbsp;
agents going after the terrorists who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:18.600
killed athletes at the 1972 Summer&nbsp;
Olympics, comes Operation Finale.

00:32:18.600 --> 00:32:22.620
The film details the early '60s&nbsp;
mission of a team of highly-trained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:28.080
top secret Israeli operatives who travel to&nbsp;
Argentina in order to bring to justice Adolf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:33.060
Eichmann. He was a high-ranking Nazi official&nbsp;
who masterminded the logistics of the Holocaust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.060 --> 00:32:37.260
responsible for the deaths of millions&nbsp;
of Jewish people. Oscar Isaac plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:41.700
real-life Mossad agent Peter Malkin,&nbsp;
leading the charge against Eichmann.

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:45.000
M.V. Moorhead of Phoenix&nbsp;
Magazine called Operation Finale…

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:49.320
"Crisp and absorbing and satisfying,&nbsp;
both as a thriller and as a moral drama."

00:32:49.920 --> 00:32:52.200
On one level, viewers know exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.200 --> 00:32:55.500
they're getting when they click on a&nbsp;
movie called Vampires vs. the Bronx.

00:32:55.500 --> 00:32:57.600
"We got vampires in the Bronx."

00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:01.620
It is indeed a violent tale of creepy&nbsp;
bloodsuckers hailing from Eastern Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:01.620 --> 00:33:06.480
in the classic vampire movie style. But this&nbsp;
time, they're in a modern-day urban setting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:12.240
specifically one of New York City's five boroughs.&nbsp;
However, Vampires vs. the Bronx is so much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:17.460
than that — it's a socially conscious celebration&nbsp;
of Bronx culture, the story of a bunch of smart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:22.380
and capable kids coming together to save the&nbsp;
day, and a deeply satirical horror-comedy.

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:25.740
The vampiric real estate agents at&nbsp;
the center of the story represent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:25.740 --> 00:33:30.180
wealthy individuals and businesses that&nbsp;
want to gentrify older neighborhoods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:34.680
and a scrappy bunch of young people have to stop&nbsp;
them before nothing is left and no one is alive.

00:33:34.680 --> 00:33:36.180
"Don't go there, Ma."

00:33:36.180 --> 00:33:38.400
"He's...he's a really bad man."

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:40.320
"Of course he is, he's in real estate."

00:33:40.320 --> 00:33:43.740
Like many critics, Ben Travis&nbsp;
of Empire had positive things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:46.860
to say about Vampires vs. the&nbsp;
Bronx. He called the film...

00:33:46.860 --> 00:33:49.620
"A witty and likable horror-comedy that manages to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:53.280
put a stake to the heart of some real&nbsp;
issues while it tickles your ribs."
 &nbsp;

00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:58.320
Based on Joe Ballarini's series of gently&nbsp;
spooky kids novels of the same name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:03.840
The Babysitter's Guide to Monster Hunting is a&nbsp;
family-friendly movie directed by Rachel Talalay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:08.700
a filmmaker best known for her work on Tank&nbsp;
Girl and the Nightmare on Elm Street series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:08.700 --> 00:34:13.980
Ballarini and Talalay were the ideal combo to&nbsp;
make what could be a kid's first horror-comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:18.840
a dark, funny, and twisted take on the&nbsp;
'80s classic Adventures in Babysitting.

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:24.420
Teenage babysitter Kelly gets a gig looking after&nbsp;
a kid on Halloween night. That's a bummer in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:29.700
of itself, made worse when the child is kidnapped&nbsp;
by the actual Boogeyman. Kelly has to do her best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:29.700 --> 00:34:33.900
to rescue the little guy. Luckily, she's helped&nbsp;
along the way in her fight against monsters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.900 --> 00:34:39.060
goblins, and pure evil by a secret society&nbsp;
of elite and well-equipped babysitters.

00:34:39.060 --> 00:34:41.700
"Ugh, I hate babysitters."

00:34:41.700 --> 00:34:44.160
Nell Minnow of Movie Mom said the film delivers...

00:34:44.160 --> 00:34:46.740
"Gorgeously imagined settings, a great cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:46.740 --> 00:34:51.840
and an exciting story that hits the exact sweet&nbsp;
spot between funny-scary and scary-funny."

00:34:52.380 --> 00:34:56.340
The best horror movies consist of a&nbsp;
lot more than just a few jump scares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:56.340 --> 00:35:00.540
and some terrifying form of bone-chilling&nbsp;
menace — the good ones reflect and comment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.540 --> 00:35:04.800
upon the everyday things that trouble human&nbsp;
beings. Little Evil is that kind of movie.

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:09.420
It's a horror-comedy, with the emphasis on&nbsp;
comedy, about an eager-to-please guy who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.420 --> 00:35:13.740
marries a woman who has a son from a previous&nbsp;
marriage. He's desperate to connect with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:17.460
inscrutable little guy so his relationship&nbsp;
can thrive and they can all come together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:17.460 --> 00:35:22.500
as a happy family. That's a difficult task,&nbsp;
as the kid is actually the spawn of Satan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.500 --> 00:35:27.420
and he's not afraid to unleash pure evil and&nbsp;
terror on his well-meaning new stepdad. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:31.740
that's where the horror comes into play; that,&nbsp;
and with a creepy hand-puppet named Reeroy.

00:35:31.740 --> 00:35:33.289
"I'm your step-father now."

00:35:33.289 --> 00:35:34.190
"Get out."

00:35:34.190 --> 00:35:40.149
"Yeah, okay. We'll just talk&nbsp;
later or whenever you want."

00:35:40.149 --> 00:35:44.580
Amanda Sink of The Hollywood Outsider&nbsp;
perfectly described the film in one line.

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:47.520
"Little Evil is [The] Omen&nbsp;
by way of Shaun of the Dead."

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:52.920
If you've never watched much Bollywood, it&nbsp;
may be hard to tell if Makkhi is making fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:52.920 --> 00:35:56.460
of those films or comfortably nestled&nbsp;
at the heart of it all. The absurdity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:56.460 --> 00:36:01.200
feels intentionally cranked to 11, but then&nbsp;
again, there's also a distinct possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:05.760
that a song-and-dance number followed by a&nbsp;
Rocky training montage for a reincarnated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:09.900
fly is completely normal. Either way,&nbsp;
it's totally worth your time to watch.

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:15.660
Here's the premise, and it's a doozy: A fly&nbsp;
born from the soul of a dead man goes on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.660 --> 00:36:20.400
revenge rampage against the guy who killed&nbsp;
him. It's John Wick crossed with A Bug's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:26.940
Life crossed with…uhhh...La-La-Land? Maybe?&nbsp;
There's a trippy Ant-Man vibe to the visuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:31.800
and explosive action sequences, all tied&nbsp;
into a deathmatch between a man and, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:31.800 --> 00:36:36.600
a housefly. As far as hidden gems go, this&nbsp;
one's like stumbling across a dead unicorn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:36.600 --> 00:36:40.860
in the woods. It's majestic. And you just&nbsp;
don't know what the hell to do with it.

00:36:40.860 --> 00:36:45.720
There seem to be two camps when it comes to&nbsp;
this Netflix animated film — those who like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:50.160
original manga hate the movie, and those who&nbsp;
haven't read the manga generally enjoyed it.

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:53.460
But love it or hate it, when it comes&nbsp;
to fresh takes on the apocalypse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:57.600
Blame! has most any other futuristic vision&nbsp;
beat, hands down. That's what makes this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:02.220
film so intriguing. In a seemingly endless,&nbsp;
continually growing city that goes for miles up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:06.600
down, and in either direction, robots&nbsp;
have taken to exterminating humans. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:06.600 --> 00:37:10.560
a small band of people has survived for&nbsp;
centuries inside an invisible shield that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:14.220
keeps the robots out. They don't know why&nbsp;
the robots can't get inside their colony;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:18.600
they just know that going outside means risking&nbsp;
life and limb at the hands of predatory robots.

00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:21.720
It's only when a stranger comes to&nbsp;
their town that they realize there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:25.140
may be a chance to do something about&nbsp;
their predicament and finally stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:25.140 --> 00:37:29.580
the expansion of the city. For vision&nbsp;
alone, Blame! is well worth watching.

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:33.480
Is the live-action version of Fullmetal&nbsp;
Alchemist as good as the anime? Not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:33.480 --> 00:37:37.620
close. Is it cheesy and hilarious and&nbsp;
full of ridiculous moments? Oh, you bet.

00:37:37.620 --> 00:37:41.100
For newcomers to the whole thing,&nbsp;
the story is about two brothers with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:41.100 --> 00:37:44.400
a natural inclination for alchemy who&nbsp;
attempt to bring their mother back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:48.360
life. Things go horribly wrong, and one&nbsp;
brother ends up with his soul trapped in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:51.900
a suit of armor while the other loses&nbsp;
an arm and a leg. When they're older,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:51.900 --> 00:37:55.860
they search for the mythical philosopher's&nbsp;
stone in the hopes of restoring their bodies.

00:37:55.860 --> 00:38:00.180
As far as anime adaptations go, Fullmetal&nbsp;
Alchemist is arguably one of the best out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:03.360
there. It doesn't cover the whole&nbsp;
story, but it serves as an origin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:03.360 --> 00:38:08.100
tale and works as a self-contained story&nbsp;
that even non-fans can enjoy. At least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:11.940
you can enjoy it if you already like over-the-top&nbsp;
action fantasies. That's all this is.

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:17.460
Maniac is hard to categorize. It's a darkly&nbsp;
comic, often surreal and lightly dystopian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:17.460 --> 00:38:22.440
miniseries set in a future world where gadgetry&nbsp;
and pharmaceuticals have rapidly progressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:27.600
but a 1980s consumer tech flavor permeates&nbsp;
the design of day-to-day life. Desperate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:32.580
guilt-ridden Annie and troubled rich family black&nbsp;
sheep Owen lock down for a heavily controlled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.580 --> 00:38:37.800
psychological drug trial at a mysterious drug&nbsp;
company operated by the eccentric Dr. James K.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:37.800 --> 00:38:43.260
Mantleray. Mantleray is literally and explicitly&nbsp;
in love with machinery who lives in the shadow of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.260 --> 00:38:47.880
his mother, Dr. Greta Mantleray, a popular&nbsp;
pop psychologist and best-selling author.

00:38:47.880 --> 00:38:52.800
That all fuses with journeys deep into the minds&nbsp;
of Owen and Annie -- and sometimes both at once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:56.880
when their subconscious realities intersect&nbsp;
-- who live out their deepest fears and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.880 --> 00:39:01.200
insecurities in disparate ways, such as&nbsp;
an exotic animal heist and a Lord of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:05.940
Rings-esque fantasy. Not only dazzling and&nbsp;
challenging, Maniac is visually stunning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:05.940 --> 00:39:10.294
psychologically dense, and often&nbsp;
surprisingly emotional all at once. .

00:39:10.294 --> 00:39:14.760
Rim of the World director McG&nbsp;
unabashedly and cheerfully made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.760 --> 00:39:19.260
an '80s-style kid adventure movie. Rim&nbsp;
of the World mixes elements of E.T.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:19.260 --> 00:39:24.960
The Goonies, and Red Dawn to create a similarly&nbsp;
fun sci-fi action movie where teens save the day.

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:28.680
"If you find yourself alone, riding&nbsp;
in green fields with the sun on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:28.680 --> 00:39:33.840
face, do not be troubled. For you are&nbsp;
in Elysium. And you are already dead."

00:39:33.840 --> 00:39:40.440
The simple, irresistible premise: four kids arrive&nbsp;
at summer camp just in time for an invasion of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:46.020
planet by wicked aliens. With the well-populated&nbsp;
"Rim of the World" camp soon abandoned, it's up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.020 --> 00:39:50.580
this ragtag group of teens who don't quite fit&nbsp;
in elsewhere to come together and thwart what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:50.580 --> 00:39:56.040
amounts to the end of the world. They'll have to&nbsp;
do it all without adults, computers, or much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.040 --> 00:40:01.134
anything… except, of course, for bikes. Heaven&nbsp;
of Horror says of this lightweight diversion:

00:40:01.134 --> 00:40:06.474
"This is an absolute treat for fans of sci-fi,&nbsp;
adventure, action, and monster movies."

00:40:07.320 --> 00:40:12.840
After bringing so much attention and accolades&nbsp;
to Netflix via his unforgettable performance as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:17.040
Sheriff Hopper on Stranger Things, it's no&nbsp;
surprise that David Harbour has free reign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:21.420
to do whatever he wants at the streaming&nbsp;
giant. And what he wants to do is a meta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.420 --> 00:40:26.040
sci-fi and horror-laced mockumentary about&nbsp;
his life, a collaboration with Arrested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:31.200
Development writer John Levenstein. The result&nbsp;
is a short special that's a bit hard to define.

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:37.800
Frankenstein's Monster's Monster, Frankenstein is&nbsp;
based around a mid-1970s made-for-TV production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:43.500
of the two-man theatrical version of Frankenstein.&nbsp;
The twist is that Harbour plays his own "father,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:43.500 --> 00:40:48.960
also an actor named David Harbour, as the&nbsp;
two generations play both Dr. Frankenstein&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:48.960 --> 00:40:53.820
and his monstrous creation at the same time. To&nbsp;
round out this tribute from the younger Harbour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:53.820 --> 00:40:58.740
to the fictional older Harbour are clips of the&nbsp;
actor from other vintage fictitious productions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.740 --> 00:41:03.780
like The Crying Detective and The Actor's Trunk.&nbsp;
The whole thing winds up both a love letter to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:03.780 --> 00:41:09.000
the craft of acting and an affectionate send-up of&nbsp;
the entire notion of playing pretend for a living.

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:14.100
Vietnam is home to a quickly developing movie&nbsp;
culture and industry, and the action thriller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.100 --> 00:41:19.800
Furie teams up two of its stars, director&nbsp;
Le-Van Kiet and actress Veronica Ngo, best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:19.800 --> 00:41:25.200
known to Western audiences as gunner Paige&nbsp;
Tico in Star Wars: The Last Jedi. In Furie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.200 --> 00:41:30.480
Ngo portrays a former criminal overlord who now&nbsp;
works as the muscle for a loan shark in the city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:30.480 --> 00:41:34.440
of Can Tho, shaking down delinquents&nbsp;
with a shattering of a kneecap or two.

00:41:34.440 --> 00:41:39.660
That violence comes home to roost when her&nbsp;
daughter, Mai, is kidnapped and seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:39.660 --> 00:41:45.000
sold to black market organ dealers. Ngo,&nbsp;
propelled by equal parts rage, guilt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:50.100
and love, takes to the seedy sides of Ho Chi&nbsp;
Minh City to get her daughter back in one piece.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:50.100 --> 00:41:54.660
Furie is a tight, often sparse ride,&nbsp;
which includes plenty of novel set pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:57.960
beautiful cinematography, and&nbsp;
an intense motorbike chase.

00:41:58.534 --> 00:42:03.180
Sometimes the scariest parts of a scary&nbsp;
movie don't come from monsters or gore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:08.100
but from menace and dread. That's what&nbsp;
propels the precisely paced Earthquake Bird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:14.280
based on the novel of the same name by Susanna&nbsp;
Jones. Alicia Vikander of Ex Machina returns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.280 --> 00:42:20.100
to the kind of moody, unsettling cinema that made&nbsp;
her famous, portraying Lucy, a Swedish expatriate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:26.280
who has settled into a life in 1989 Tokyo that is&nbsp;
repetitive, bland, and mundane. It's by design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:31.080
as she's looking to flee from and forget&nbsp;
past traumas. She works as a translator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:31.080 --> 00:42:35.460
living alone while maintaining some low-key&nbsp;
friendships and playing cello in a string quartet.

00:42:35.460 --> 00:42:39.060
One day, Lucy meets Teiji, a noodle shop worker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:39.060 --> 00:42:43.440
and aspiring photographer. She enchants&nbsp;
him and lets him make portraits of her.

00:42:43.440 --> 00:42:47.700
"I've never had my picture&nbsp;
taken before. Not properly."

00:42:48.960 --> 00:42:50.760
"Then, I'm the first."

00:42:50.760 --> 00:42:54.000
But just when it seems like her&nbsp;
icy veneer is starting to thaw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:57.540
Lucy's life is interrupted by&nbsp;
a brash American named Lily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:57.540 --> 00:43:02.580
who goes missing and is presumed dead. Is&nbsp;
the mysterious Lucy responsible, either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:08.640
via conscious act or her status as a tragedy&nbsp;
magnet? Or is she merely an unreliable narrator?

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:11.700
Gabriel Silver of the Detroit Metro Times said:

00:43:11.700 --> 00:43:14.640
"Earthquake Bird is an enjoyable neo-noir film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:18.180
with enough twists and turns to leave&nbsp;
you thinking long after the ending."

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:24.660
According to the thoughtful sci-fi movie Io,&nbsp;
humanity is doomed to never change its ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.660 --> 00:43:28.920
and in the not-too-distant future, Earth&nbsp;
becomes uninhabitable. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:34.920
scientists have found a new home for mankind&nbsp;
on Io, one of Jupiter's many moons. A scientist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:39.300
named Sam is among the handful of humans&nbsp;
who haven't made the migration to Io yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:43.440
staying behind to test the water and&nbsp;
air for signs of decreasing toxicity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:48.060
as well as to raise bees that she hopes&nbsp;
could someday pollinate and help save the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:48.060 --> 00:43:52.800
planet's vegetation. Time is running out,&nbsp;
and certain catastrophic events lead Sam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:52.800 --> 00:43:57.000
to head with another survivor, Micah, to&nbsp;
a shuttle that will take them into space.

00:43:57.000 --> 00:44:00.600
Io is full of philosophical and mental tension as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:00.600 --> 00:44:04.560
these two bear witness to what could&nbsp;
be Earth's final, extinctive whimper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.560 --> 00:44:10.080
but also a newfound hope for the future.&nbsp;
Leigh Monson of Birth.Movies.Death. says:

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:15.240
"If you're looking for science fiction that&nbsp;
makes manifest the anxieties of a dying world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.240 --> 00:44:18.780
our dying world, then Io&nbsp;
is a good place to start."

00:44:18.780 --> 00:44:26.040
"We weren't ready for this. No matter what&nbsp;
warning we got from people like your father."
 &nbsp;

00:44:26.880 --> 00:44:31.620
What if John Wick was more violent, more&nbsp;
nihilistic, and with a gleeful undercurrent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.620 --> 00:44:36.660
of shock humor and an honest exploration of the&nbsp;
mental effects of a violent life? That's Polar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:41.580
the messy story of one man's quest to relax&nbsp;
and secure his pension. Mads Mikkelsen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:41.580 --> 00:44:46.020
the Danish actor best known for his starring&nbsp;
role on the stylish TV version of Hannibal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.020 --> 00:44:51.840
stars as Duncan Vizla, a highly effective assassin&nbsp;
forced by his corporate bosses to retire at age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:56.520
50 and enjoy his golden years. Of course, his&nbsp;
company plans to kill him off to avoid funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:56.520 --> 00:45:01.800
his payout. From a lovely cabin in Montana, Duncan&nbsp;
has to fight off some violent attacks while also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:01.800 --> 00:45:05.820
dealing with PTSD and forging a friendship&nbsp;
with a neighbor with demons of her own.
 &nbsp;

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:12.480
Groundhog Day may seem like a comedy, but it's&nbsp;
actually a horror movie. Seriously: A guy is such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:12.480 --> 00:45:16.800
a nasty person that the universe spontaneously&nbsp;
conspires to make him shape up by sticking him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:16.800 --> 00:45:21.540
in a time loop from which he cannot escape.&nbsp;
That's a living nightmare. ARQ takes Groundhog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:21.540 --> 00:45:26.880
Day for the horror movie it is and runs with it.&nbsp;
Robbie Amell stars as an engineer responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:26.880 --> 00:45:31.380
for a world-changing technology, only to find&nbsp;
himself dealing with a home invasion brought on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:31.380 --> 00:45:36.720
by bad dudes who want his technology for their own&nbsp;
means. Our protagonist gets stuck in a time loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:36.720 --> 00:45:42.180
forcing him to experience his horrible situation&nbsp;
over and over again, until he can find a way out.

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:47.160
"Today is repeating. Tell me you&nbsp;
remember. Come on, you have to remember."

00:45:49.620 --> 00:45:51.501
"Have you lost your fucking f—ing mind?"

00:45:51.501 --> 00:45:55.147
Life in an especially dingy and crime-filled 

00:45:55.147 --> 00:45:59.640
part on London is rough for Tom, a shy and&nbsp;
awkward teenager who just wants to go about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:59.640 --> 00:46:03.780
his business and maybe take things to the next&nbsp;
level with his friend Lucy. When he goes over to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.780 --> 00:46:08.760
her apartment to study together, he's greeted&nbsp;
by thugs who have just savagely attacked Lucy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:13.080
and her brother. Tom runs away and tries to&nbsp;
call the police, only for the thugs to shoot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:13.080 --> 00:46:17.640
him in the head and leave him for dead.&nbsp;
The good news: He survived! The bad news:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:17.640 --> 00:46:22.980
He wakes up two days after the attack with bits&nbsp;
of his phone stuck inside in his severely damaged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:27.720
noggin. Luckily, like some kind of Silicon Valley&nbsp;
Spider-Man, the advanced computing technology in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:27.720 --> 00:46:32.280
the phone merges with Tom's brain to give&nbsp;
him superpowers. With those great powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:32.280 --> 00:46:37.140
he takes it as his responsibility to track&nbsp;
down the bad guys who nearly killed Lucy. 

00:46:37.860 --> 00:46:43.200
What if Baby Driver wasn't a soundtrack-driven&nbsp;
action comedy, and was instead an unpredictable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:43.200 --> 00:46:48.000
intricately plotted suspense thriller about a&nbsp;
getaway driver having his worst ever day of work?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.000 --> 00:46:53.880
That's the logline for Wheelman, which also feels&nbsp;
like the greatest Jason Statham movie that Statham&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:53.880 --> 00:46:59.160
never made. Frank Grillo portrays the unnamed,&nbsp;
unflappable wheelman in a bank heist scheme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:59.160 --> 00:47:04.020
just out of prison and in debt to a Boston crime&nbsp;
family. Everything that could go wrong does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:08.340
but it's not all that funny — it's stressful&nbsp;
enough driving a getaway car for a robbery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:08.340 --> 00:47:12.480
and it's even more nerve-wracking for the&nbsp;
wheelman when he gets more or less carjacked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:12.480 --> 00:47:16.980
by another band of mysterious criminals. Much&nbsp;
of the action takes place inside the car and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:16.980 --> 00:47:21.360
inside the head of the wheelman, speeding along&nbsp;
from one high-pressure situation to another.

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:28.680
James Badge Dale plays Clyne, one of the smartest&nbsp;
people in the world. Clyne is a DARPA scientist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:34.080
employed by the U.S. military to develop weapons&nbsp;
technology to use in the ongoing Moldovan War in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:34.080 --> 00:47:38.460
Eastern Europe. He's summoned to the front lines&nbsp;
to consult on some strange findings captured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:38.460 --> 00:47:43.500
by one of his best gadgets, hyper-spectral&nbsp;
goggles. It would seem some troops wearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:43.500 --> 00:47:48.000
the goggles spotted some ghostly figures out in&nbsp;
the wild that tend to kill whatever they come in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:52.440
contact with. Before the military can report to&nbsp;
their higher-ups, they need Clyne to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:52.440 --> 00:47:57.600
just what it is, and so off he goes into the war&nbsp;
zone to stalk the beast alongside a special ops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:57.600 --> 00:48:02.340
team and a C.I.A. operative. What they find&nbsp;
is way worse and more complicated than they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:02.340 --> 00:48:07.140
thought possible. A harrowing war movie with&nbsp;
elements of District 9 and Predator thrown in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:07.140 --> 00:48:10.901
Spectral is clever, intense, and worth a watch.

00:48:10.901 --> 00:48:14.324
Yes, there's a sequel to 2000's Crouching Tiger, 

00:48:14.400 --> 00:48:19.260
Hidden Dragon. And, like so many other revivals&nbsp;
of classic properties, it's just hanging out on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.260 --> 00:48:24.240
Netflix. Loosely based on the same "Crane-Iron"&nbsp;
series of books by Wang Dulu as the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:24.240 --> 00:48:29.280
Crouching Tiger, Sword of Destiny builds on its&nbsp;
predecessor's magical combination of folk tales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:29.280 --> 00:48:33.660
multi-faceted characters, and physically&nbsp;
impossible acrobatic fight scenes. Michelle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:38.700
Yeoh returns as You Shu Lien, fresh out of&nbsp;
exile and locked in an important battle with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.700 --> 00:48:42.840
the West Lotus clan for control of the&nbsp;
Green Destiny sword. Directing Sword of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:42.840 --> 00:48:47.160
Destiny is Woo-Ping Yuen, the martial arts&nbsp;
choreographer on the first Crouching Tiger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:47.160 --> 00:48:51.840
and he certainly brings his prowess for creating&nbsp;
gorgeous and soaring aerial combat sequences.

00:48:53.040 --> 00:48:57.120
Why are period horror films so much&nbsp;
scarier than ones set in the modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:57.120 --> 00:49:01.680
day? Is it because the relative ignorance and&nbsp;
brutality of the past adds an extra element&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:01.680 --> 00:49:06.240
of terror? Or are images of Victorian-era&nbsp;
Londoners in bleak clothes living bleak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:06.240 --> 00:49:10.560
lives inherently unsettling? Probably&nbsp;
that, although blood-hungry monsters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:10.560 --> 00:49:15.960
freaky islands, and blood cults are scary too.&nbsp;
Fortunately, Apostle possesses all those things.

00:49:15.960 --> 00:49:22.200
Set in 1905, Apostle concerns a man named Thomas,&nbsp;
who leaves London for an island run by a cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.200 --> 00:49:27.360
that's kidnapped his sister. Thomas poses as a new&nbsp;
adherent to gain favor with the cult members and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:31.680
their leader, in the hopes of rescuing his sister&nbsp;
from them. However, things get awfully sticky for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:31.680 --> 00:49:36.840
Thomas when he realizes that the cult's teachings&nbsp;
aren't exactly wrong. Imagine if Little Shop of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:36.840 --> 00:49:41.820
Horrors mashed up with The Wicker Man, only&nbsp;
ten times as freaky, and you'll get Apostle.

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:47.160
There's always room for a new twist and&nbsp;
new voices in even the most trope-heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:47.160 --> 00:49:51.600
film genres. Take Cargo, which revives&nbsp;
the zombie movie genre from its funk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:51.600 --> 00:49:56.040
by adding new dimensions of humanity.&nbsp;
Directors Yolanda Ramke and Ben Howling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:56.040 --> 00:50:00.540
based the film on their 2013 short of the&nbsp;
same name. It follows a man named Andy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:00.540 --> 00:50:05.280
played by Martin Freeman, who, with his wife&nbsp;
and infant daughter, preserves himself up in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:09.780
houseboat along a river deep in the interior&nbsp;
of Australia following a zombie apocalypse.

00:50:09.780 --> 00:50:14.700
Andy wants to take the boat upriver to the&nbsp;
safety of military protection; his wife wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:14.700 --> 00:50:19.080
to make a supply run. Eventually, Andy heads&nbsp;
out on land with Rosie strapped to his back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:19.080 --> 00:50:24.240
fighting off the undead. Ultimately, Cargo&nbsp;
is a film about the will to not just survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:24.240 --> 00:50:27.180
but to protect your loved&nbsp;
ones, no matter the cost.
 &nbsp;

00:50:28.260 --> 00:50:34.440
I Am Mother, the feature debut by writer-director&nbsp;
Grant Sputore, takes place after an apocalyptic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:34.440 --> 00:50:38.940
extinction-level event has wiped out all&nbsp;
of humanity. Fortunately, a repopulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:38.940 --> 00:50:44.400
contingency plan springs into action: a robot&nbsp;
named Mother activates in a secure lab facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:44.400 --> 00:50:51.180
and raises one of 63,000 human embryos pre-frozen&nbsp;
by scientists should life ever cease to exist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:51.180 --> 00:50:56.220
Mother then raises her human daughter, helpfully&nbsp;
named Daughter, through a pleasant childhood…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.220 --> 00:51:00.900
until things get prickly during the teen&nbsp;
years. Daughter discovers a living mouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:00.900 --> 00:51:06.000
and wonders if there's more life beyond her four&nbsp;
walls. But then she finds out there really is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:11.220
when an adult human woman comes knocking at&nbsp;
the facility door. I Am Mother is ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:11.220 --> 00:51:16.260
a thrilling, chilling, and even oddly touching&nbsp;
allegory about parenthood and letting go.

00:51:17.044 --> 00:51:22.080
The Wandering Earth is one of the highest-grossing&nbsp;
movies of the decade… but most North American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.080 --> 00:51:28.020
movie fans probably have no idea it even exists.&nbsp;
It enjoyed an extremely small release in the U.S.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:28.020 --> 00:51:33.540
but earned nearly $700 million at China's&nbsp;
box office. The film is one of that country's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:33.540 --> 00:51:38.580
first Hollywood-style, big-budget, blockbuster&nbsp;
science-fiction epics. In The Wandering Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:38.580 --> 00:51:43.320
scientists discover that the sun is evolving&nbsp;
into a red giant, meaning that it's expanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:43.320 --> 00:51:48.720
at a rate that will devour Earth in 100 years&nbsp;
and the entire solar system within 300 years.

00:51:48.720 --> 00:51:53.040
The globe's governments and scientists work&nbsp;
together and come up with a creative solution:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:53.040 --> 00:51:58.800
10,000 gigantic jet engines are installed&nbsp;
around the world, propelling the planet out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:58.800 --> 00:52:04.200
of its orbit to begin a very long journey to a new&nbsp;
permanent resting spot more than four lightyears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:04.200 --> 00:52:09.300
away. Things go wrong relatively early when&nbsp;
spaceship Earth gets caught in Jupiter's orbit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:13.980
which will ensure its eventual destruction —&nbsp;
if our heroes can't get the jets working again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:13.980 --> 00:52:19.440
that is. Comparable to Armageddon, The&nbsp;
Core, and Interstellar, The Wandering Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:19.440 --> 00:52:22.801
is a fun and accessible sci-fi adventure.

00:52:22.801 --> 00:52:25.754
Filmmakers can use the limitless tools and 

00:52:25.800 --> 00:52:30.720
imagination of science fiction to say something&nbsp;
about the world, and Stefon Bristol explores some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:36.120
powerful themes with See You Yesterday. Bristol&nbsp;
and his talented young cast combine sci-fi tropes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:40.920
with brutal social commentary — this time-travel&nbsp;
thriller is about the future and the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:40.920 --> 00:52:44.880
but really it's about the present.&nbsp;
C.J., played by Eden Duncan-Smith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:44.880 --> 00:52:49.140
is a precocious and preternaturally gifted&nbsp;
young scientist who, with her best friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:49.140 --> 00:52:53.940
and collaborator Sebastian, has constructed a&nbsp;
rudimentary — but functional — time machine.

00:52:53.940 --> 00:52:58.440
They just want to go back in time by a&nbsp;
day to look around and then come back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:58.440 --> 00:53:03.240
so as not to "butterfly effect" the past and&nbsp;
rewrite history. But See You Yesterday is set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:03.240 --> 00:53:06.480
in the predominantly African-American&nbsp;
Brooklyn neighborhood of Flatbush,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:06.480 --> 00:53:12.000
and C.J's brother Calvin is horrifyingly shot&nbsp;
down in the street by the police. C.J. has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:12.000 --> 00:53:16.260
use her device to go back and attempt to save&nbsp;
her brother's life while battling against the the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:16.260 --> 00:53:21.780
time loops and complications she both causes and&nbsp;
encounters. See You Yesterday is a heartbreaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:21.780 --> 00:53:26.520
thoughtful movie, one that's laced with great&nbsp;
sci-fi references as well as the Jamaican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:26.520 --> 00:53:30.960
influence that permeates Flatbush culture.&nbsp;
Don't waste any more time — see this movie.

